#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# `bin/copy-to-cloud` — copy one business's site from the LOCAL builder to the
# DEPLOYED one (REQ-289).
#
# WHY IT IS NOT CALLED `publish`. `publish` already names an operation — take a
# draft, freeze it as a version, make that version live. The push script this
# replaced borrowed the word for a different one on a different axis: copy bytes
# from this laptop to Cloudflare. Worse, it copied the wrong thing. It read the
# git-tracked file tier; the sites that actually exist were authored in the
# builder and live in the D1 and R2 store under
# `apps/control-app/.wrangler/state/`. So until this command there was no way to
# move a builder-authored site anywhere at all. REQ-290 retired both the script
# and the tier it read.
#
# THE DIRECTION IS IN THE NAME. `bin/copy-from-cloud` is the same two HTTP calls
# with the ends swapped. It is two names rather than one command with a flag
# because the operator reads the name back later, out of a shell history or a
# runbook, and has to know which way the bytes went.
#
# THE WORKER READS, AND THE WORKER WRITES. Node cannot open the source: under
# `wrangler dev` a D1 database is a miniflare SQLite file whose layout is an
# implementation detail, and R2 is a second SQLite plus a blob directory beside
# it. So this is two HTTP calls — `GET /api/export` at one end, `POST /api/import`
# at the other — and each end reads and writes through the very store it serves
# from. That was the push script's own argument for why an import goes over HTTP,
# applied here in reverse to the read.
#
# THE TARGET BUSINESS MUST ALREADY EXIST. Copying to a business that is not on
# the far side fails, naming what that side does offer. This command never mints
# a tenant: a deployment acquiring a business nobody signed up for, from a laptop
# script, is the failure that refusal exists to prevent.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/copy-to-cloud "Lagrange Foundry"
#   bin/copy-to-cloud --origin http://127.0.0.1:8799 "Lagrange Foundry"
#   bin/copy-to-cloud --force "Lagrange Foundry"
#   bin/copy-to-cloud --backup storage/backups/lagrange-foundry.json "Lagrange Foundry"
#
# <business> is the business's NAME as it reads in the builder. It resolves to a
# different id on each side, independently, so the name is the only thing that
# means the same on both.
#
# --origin overrides the LOCAL end (default http://localhost:8788), so the pair
# works against a dev server on any port — in practice `bin/access-sim`, which is
# the local front door that can reach a business other than TENANT_ID.
#
# --force is BUG-51's answer. `/api/import` refuses a target carrying changes
# authored in the builder, naming the business and the change count; nothing is
# written without it.
#
# --backup FILE writes the SOURCE side's export to FILE and touches the
# destination not at all. To-cloud reads local, so this is the local builder's
# own export, landing somewhere committable.
#
# --site is the default and the only class implemented. --contacts is recognised
# and refused: see `bin/copy-from-cloud` for why the two directions answer
# differently.
#
# The cloud end is behind Cloudflare Access and needs the service-token PAIR
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID + CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET. Provision one with
# `bin/access-token`. CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
# is an API credential for api.cloudflare.com and is refused here like no
# credential at all. See apps/control-app/ACCESS.md.
#
# Exit codes: 0 success; non-zero anything else.

set -euo pipefail

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
repo_root="$(cd "$here/.." && pwd)"

if [[ "${1:-}" == "-h" || "${1:-}" == "--help" ]]; then
  sed -n '2,66p' "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//'
  exit 0
fi

# EVERY FLAG IS FORWARDED RATHER THAN RE-PARSED HERE. The refusals this command
# owes the operator — half a credential pair, an unknown business, --contacts,
# BUG-51's 409 — are one implementation in `copy.ts`, shared with
# `bin/copy-from-cloud`. A second parser in bash would be a second place for one
# of them to drift.
exec "$repo_root/bin/1c" copy-to-cloud "$@"
